      "writtenOn": "2020-06-22T12:37:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why is this needed?\n\nAlso why is kernel_write needed?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 623,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 623,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "06eaafd2351a27e6f396eb1a762ba0879e38efb3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "976332b2_ab395529",
        "filename": "abi_gki_aarch64_exynos_whitelist",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 623,
      "author": {
        "id": 1718267
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-24T03:35:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is because vfs_read \u0026 vfs_write removed, and it replaced by kernel_read / kernel_write at driver side : \n\nbelow is code change:\n\n-               nread \u003d vfs_read(fp, (char __user *)fw_data, fw_size, \u0026fp-\u003ef_pos);\n+               nread \u003d kernel_read(fp, (char __user *)fw_data, fw_size, \u0026fp-\u003ef_pos);\n\n\n-                       ret \u003d vfs_write(filp, write_req-\u003ebuf, write_req-\u003eactual,\n+                       ret \u003d kernel_write(filp, write_req-\u003ebuf, write_req-\u003eactual,",
      "parentUuid": "278316f9_8e2690ba",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 623,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 623,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "06eaafd2351a27e6f396eb1a762ba0879e38efb3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e0b8241b_ebc4442c",
        "filename": "abi_gki_aarch64_exynos_whitelist",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 623,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-24T06:03:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No driver should be reading/writing to files directly, please use the proper userspace apis instead.",
      "parentUuid": "976332b2_ab395529",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 623,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 623,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "06eaafd2351a27e6f396eb1a762ba0879e38efb3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c36bf44b_f6751048",
        "filename": "abi_gki_aarch64_exynos_whitelist",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 623,
      "author": {
        "id": 1718267
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-25T23:11:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So many kernel driver (including mainline code) are using this function.\nAnd more_over, our other 3rd party driver also use it. we need that kernel_read / write function for driver action.\n\nAs you know, we escaped from vfs_read / write function but without kernel_read() or kernel_write which API can we use?\nIf possible, please give us some hint.\n\nbelow is chaning log jump to kernel_read in mainline.\n\ncommit 74a6565f378c1b71f2572bf1f3c03ad35a7c75ca\nAuthor: Jann Horn \u003cjannh@google.com\u003e\nDate:   Fri Mar 1 21:11:24 2019 +0100\n\n    staging: rtl8723bs: use kernel_read() instead of open-coded version\n\n    Replace the manual call to f_op-\u003eread() under KERNEL_DS with kernel_read().\n    This also reduces the number of users of the legacy alias get_ds() for\n    KERNEL_DS.\n\n    Signed-off-by: Jann Horn \u003cjannh@google.com\u003e\n    Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman \u003cgregkh@linuxfoundation.org\u003e",
      "parentUuid": "e0b8241b_ebc4442c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 623,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 623,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "06eaafd2351a27e6f396eb1a762ba0879e38efb3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "880217de_8a5cb8e4",
        "filename": "abi_gki_aarch64_exynos_whitelist",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 623,
      "author": {
        "id": 1670210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-26T01:15:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When debugging any driver using any hardware that can process large data like DMA, sub-core or etc, internal buffer dump or debugging data injection is very essential.\nIf kernel_read/write() is allowed, it is very simple. Just insert kernel_read/write() in wanted place with if statement which checks debugging flag.\nBut, if buffer dump/injection should be created without kernel_read/write(), following code is needed.\n\n1. Make a file in anyfs.\n2. Create file read/write operation.\n3. Read or copy the file during testing.\n\nBut, upper approach has following problems.\n\n1. If DMA buffer is very small for any reason (often latency problem) and interrupt is very often, read from user space can easily miss any single transmission. To evade this situation more implementation like copying each transmission to other large buffer is needed.\n2. To read or write the file, user process needs session like ADB shell. If it isn\u0027t available for any reason, user space program for debugging also should be implemented.\n\nIf kernel_read/write() is allowed, many driver developers can save many times for debugging.",
      "parentUuid": "c36bf44b_f6751048",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 623,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 623,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "06eaafd2351a27e6f396eb1a762ba0879e38efb3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
